# PART 8 — FUTURE APPLICATION INTEGRATION ARCHITECTURE

Prepare the backend to support the way we intend to build and add ICY applications.

The development process will be:

1. Build an individual ICY application.
2. Complete its interface and functionality.
3. Build and test its own required backend.
4. Export the application project as a ZIP file.
5. Upload that ZIP into the existing ICY development workflow.
6. Instruct the AI development environment to inspect and integrate the application with ICY App Launcher.
7. Register the application through the launcher integration architecture.
8. Test its account connection and launch behavior.
9. Confirm that it appears correctly in ICY App Launcher.
10. Repeat for the next application.

Build a stable application integration contract so all future applications follow the same rules.

### Application integration requirements

Every future ICY app should be capable of:

- Identifying the signed-in ICY Account user through supported authentication.
- Obtaining its authorized user identity.
- Using its own application-level permissions.
- Connecting to ICY Cloud when required.
- Registering its application metadata.
- Providing a supported launch destination or integration identifier.
- Returning useful launch and integration errors.
- Maintaining its own application data and backend responsibilities.

Applications should not need separate user accounts when the existing ICY Account SSO architecture supports them.

Do not merge all individual app backends into the ICY App Launcher Backend.

The launcher backend manages the launcher and coordinates approved integrations. Individual applications remain independently maintainable products.

### ZIP package security

A ZIP file must never be treated as automatically safe executable software.

Do not blindly execute uploaded ZIP contents.

Create or document a future secure package-inspection and integration procedure that considers:

- File validation.
- Package metadata validation.
- Dependency review.
- File-path traversal protection.
- Untrusted script handling.
- Malware scanning where supported.
- Deployment configuration.
- Actual application destination.
- Integration permissions.

Do not allow untrusted packages to execute arbitrary backend commands.

The launcher may reference an approved application's actual deployment location or native launch identifier without hosting or executing its code inside the launcher backend.

